  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: Out of consideration for neighbours with allergies, the Tenant agrees not to keep any animal in the unit.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:09:09.464766Z",
 "text": "No, this clause is void and cannot be enforced. \n\nUnder **section 14** of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, any provision in a tenancy agreement prohibiting the presence of animals in or about the residential complex is void. Including a justification in the text—such as protecting neighbours with allergies—does not override the Act or make a \"no pets\" lease clause valid, even if the tenant signs and agrees to it. (While a landlord has separate remedies under the Act to seek eviction if a specific animal *actually* causes a serious allergic reaction during the tenancy, they cannot enforce a blanket ban in the lease itself.)\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 14",
